Open Bilingual Translate — data handling

This extension reads and modifies web-page text to show bilingual translations. Text selected by the translation logic, and existing video caption text when the optional subtitle feature is enabled, are sent to the translation provider you choose.

Legacy providers include Google and Yandex; inherited translation code also contains Bing/DeepL support. AI providers send text directly to the configured DeepSeek or compatible endpoint. A loopback endpoint can be used for local model inference; the model server's own data handling remains under your control. This project does not operate a translation relay server.

AI API credentials and settings are stored in chrome.storage.local in the browser profile. They are not included in normal configuration exports and are not intentionally injected into the web page. Browser extension storage is not an OS-encrypted credential vault. Restoring default settings removes the saved AI profiles.

New page/paragraph and subtitle translation paths use bounded in-memory caches, with no disk translation-cache reads or writes. Incognito requests bypass shared reading and AI caches. Inherited tools may still use the legacy Google/Yandex persistent cache. Use browser profile controls to remove local extension data.

When subtitle translation is enabled, the extension can read existing captions, observe YouTube's own caption responses, and load course WebVTT tracks from video.deeplearning.ai. Site-specific page-world bridges only transfer caption metadata/text and cannot access extension credentials. A readable timeline permits limited advance translation; this can send upcoming caption text to the selected provider before it is displayed. The extension does not capture audio or bypass course login requirements.

Provider privacy policies, network access and API charges depend on the provider you select. Links to external translation, documentation or original-author support sites are governed by those sites' policies.

Source: https://github.com/Yvonson/immersive-translate

The optional Windows local dictionary uses Chrome Native Messaging to query installed SQLite definition dictionaries. The lookup host has no network client in its query path, listens on no HTTP port and has no startup service. Definitions, IPA and examples stay local; no lookup history is created. A selected word may briefly appear in an extension URL fragment, which is removed after opening the dictionary page.

Pronunciation is separate and only starts when requested. The default browser TTS provider uses an available voice of the chosen accent; voices are labeled local or remote. Remote voices send the requested word/phrase to their speech engine. Optional Google Cloud TTS sends only that word/phrase and voice settings directly to texttospeech.googleapis.com; API fees and provider policies apply. Errors never trigger an automatic switch to another provider. Audio is played from memory and is not retained as a recording collection.

The Google Cloud key is stored separately in chrome.storage.local, excluded from ordinary configuration export, never returned in settings responses, and not sent in URLs or page-world messages. Storage is not an OS-encrypted vault. Delete the key in dictionary settings, or remove the extension's local data. Selection length is limited to 120 characters/8 words. Closing a card or changing a query cancels pending work and stops its playback.

Initial definition resource preparation is an explicit developer download command; import, verification and ordinary queries do not download data. nativeMessaging connects the registered local program; tts accesses browser voices; offscreen plays requested Cloud audio in an extension document. No microphone or audio capture permission is added. Resource data is kept under the current user's LocalAppData by default and retained when uninstalling the companion unless explicitly deleted.
